# Block Ownership Model, Phase 2 (Fork A): the new IR shape a block/jump
# engine runs on, and the smallest possible accessors for it -- see
# docs/plans/block-ownership-model.md section 3.1-3.2 and section 6, Fork A.
#
# IR shape:
#   ["BlockProgramIR", entry_block_name, [blocks]]
#   ["BlockIR", name, [params], [instrs]]
#
# Deliberately NOT today's ["FuncIR", name, params, instrs, lines] shape
# (self_hosting/lib/lower.patlang) even though it looks similar -- no
# `lines` table yet (debugger parity is Phase 8's own job, not this one),
# and semantically a BlockIR is entered by a JUMP that populates exactly
# its own params from a caller-supplied pointer, never a CALL that pushes a
# stack frame. There is no call stack in this engine at all (Fork A,
# "turtles all the way down") -- see interp.patlang's own header for what
# that means for how a program's control flow actually threads through.
#
# Instructions this phase's restricted subset actually emits (a small,
# named subset of what the real lower.patlang supports, not a
# reinterpretation of any of them):
#   ["Const", "num"|"str"|"bool", text]
#   ["Load", name]              ["Store", name]
#   ["Bin", op]                 ["Un", op]
#   ["Jump", n]                 ["JumpIfFalse", n]   -- WITHIN one block only
#   ["Print"]                   -- pops and prints one value; this phase's
#                                  only "host call", standing in for a full
#                                  CallHost dispatch (out of scope here --
#                                  see lower.patlang's own header)
#   ["JumpBlock", target_name, [param_names]]
#                                -- pops one value per param_name (in that
#                                   order), builds a FRESH pointer holding
#                                   only those name->value pairs, and
#                                   transfers control to target_name's own
#                                   BlockIR at instruction 0. Nothing from
#                                   the current block's own locals survives
#                                   this jump except what's explicitly
#                                   listed -- interp.patlang enforces this
#                                   by construction, not by convention (see
#                                   its own header).
#
# Phase 3 adds (section 3.4/3.5, Fork D wired in for real):
#   ["BoxNew"]                  -- pops a value, allocates a refcounted
#                                   1-value "Box" (heap.patlang's bm_alloc),
#                                   pushes its pointer
#   ["BoxGet"]                  -- pops a Box pointer, pushes its value
#   ["BoxSet"]                  -- pops (value, ptr); mutates ptr's cell in
#                                   place if bm_rc_touch_for_mutation says
#                                   refcount 1, else allocates a fresh cell
#                                   (clone-on-write) and pushes THAT
#                                   pointer -- the caller must re-Store it,
#                                   exactly like list_set's own
#                                   "reassign to the result" convention
#   ["BoxShare"]                 -- pops a Box pointer, bm_rc_inc's it, and
#                                   pushes the SAME pointer back: the one
#                                   explicit "make another reference" op
#                                   this phase supports (see lower.patlang's
#                                   own header for why this isn't automatic
#                                   at every value copy yet)
#   ["ContractFail", message]   -- a RUNTIME guaranteed-fail check (via
#                                   contract_check), only when this
#                                   instruction is actually reached --
#                                   matches section 3.4's own original
#                                   template and self_hosting/lib/lower
#                                   .patlang's real immutable-reassignment
#                                   check exactly, at block-model scale
#
# Phase 4 adds (section 3.3/6, Fork B wired in for real -- full
# elimination of ambient globals):
#   ["GlobalGet"]                -- pops (globals_val, key); pushes the
#                                    matching value (or false if absent)
#   ["GlobalSet"]                -- pops (globals_val, key, value); writes
#                                    the UPDATED globals value directly
#                                    into the "__globals" local (see
#                                    lower.patlang's own header on why this
#                                    one instruction, uniquely, writes a
#                                    local directly rather than pushing --
#                                    it's what makes set_global(...) look
#                                    like an ordinary ambient call from the
#                                    source's own point of view, exactly
#                                    section 3.3's stated goal)
#   ["HandlerNew"]                -- pushes a fresh, empty handler value
#                                    (an ordinary assoc-list, no refcounted
#                                    heap involved -- a deliberate object
#                                    handler is explicit program STATE, not
#                                    a mutation-exclusivity concern)
#   ["HandlerRegister"]           -- pops (handler, name, ref); pushes an
#                                    UPDATED handler (caller re-Stores it,
#                                    same "reassign to the result"
#                                    convention as BoxSet/list_set)
#   ["HandlerLookup"]             -- pops (handler, name); pushes the
#                                    matching ref, or false if absent
#
# Phase 7 adds (section 6, Fork C wired in for real):
#   ["BoxSetUnchecked"]          -- same effect as BoxSet, but skips
#                                    bm_rc_touch_for_mutation's own runtime
#                                    check entirely and always mutates in
#                                    place -- only ever emitted by
#                                    lower.patlang's own flight check
#                                    (flight_check.patlang) at a box_set
#                                    call site it has PROVEN, statically,
#                                    can only ever see refcount 1. See
#                                    flight_check.patlang's own header for
#                                    exactly how narrow that proof is kept.
#
# Phase 10 adds event dispatch (design doc section 8's own claim, checked
# directly against rust-runtime/src/ir/interpreter.rs BEFORE building this
# and found to be WRONG as stated: `emit` is not a one-way jump, it's a
# real call-with-return -- each registered handler runs via run_function,
# then control resumes at the emit() call site. This engine has no call
# stack (Fork A), so Emit is a narrowly-scoped, explicit exception: a
# bounded sub-execution of exactly one handler block at a time, which the
# lowerer guarantees can never itself contain a JumpBlock (see
# lower.patlang's own Phase 10 note), so there is nothing to unwind.
#   ["Emit"]                     -- pops (globals, event_name, payload),
#                                    looks up every handler block name
#                                    registered against event_name in the
#                                    program's own event table
#                                    (bi_program_events, below), runs each
#                                    one in registration order as a bounded
#                                    sub-execution (interp.patlang's own
#                                    bi_run_block, called recursively --
#                                    see its header for why this is safe),
#                                    threading each handler's own possibly-
#                                    updated "__globals" into the next, and
#                                    writes the final result back into the
#                                    CURRENT block's own "__globals" local
#                                    before falling through to the next
#                                    instruction. Never ends the current
#                                    block the way JumpBlock does.
#
# BlockProgramIR gains a 4th element as of Phase 10:
#   ["BlockProgramIR", entry_block_name, [blocks], events]
# where `events` is `[[event_name, [handler_block_name, ...]], ...]`, in
# registration (source) order -- a compile-time table, deliberately
# narrower than the real engine's runtime `register_event_handler` (which
# lets a handler close over arbitrary outer-scope locals as a genuine
# closure -- this engine never built first-class closures, see
# lower.patlang's own Phase 6 note, so that's out of reach here without
# first generalizing loop-body free-variable capture into real closure
# values, which this phase does not attempt). Named plainly as a v1
# restriction, not silently assumed equivalent to production `when`
# semantics.
#
# "__globals" is a HIDDEN param every block gets automatically (added by
# lower.patlang, never written in a function's own `takes` clause) and
# every JumpBlock forwards automatically -- see lower.patlang's own header
# for exactly how "the PatLang source someone actually wrote never
# changes" (section 3.3's own claim) is kept true here. A handler value,
# by contrast, is an ORDINARY value a program passes around explicitly
# like any other -- the concrete difference section 6/Fork B draws between
# "truly program-wide" (globals) and "deliberately, narrowly scoped"
# (a handler passed as a plain parameter).
#
# `mut` parameter syntax (section 3.4) doesn't exist in
# self_hosting/lib/parser.patlang today, and adding it there is out of
# scope (the "fully parallel, don't edit the existing pipeline" decision)
# -- checked directly rather than assumed, see lower.patlang's own header.
# A source-level NAME CONVENTION stands in for it here: a parameter named
# with a `mut_` prefix (e.g. `mut_counter`) is treated as `mut`; any other
# parameter is read-only for BoxSet purposes. A real syntax extension is
# later work if this design is ever adopted into the actual grammar, not
# something this phase invents on its own authority.
#
# Phase 18 adds (the generic host-call/list-literal work Phase 17 named
# and deferred, now filled in):
#   ["BuildList", n]             -- pops n values (pushed in WRITTEN
#                                    order by a literal `[a, b, c]`),
#                                    pushes a fresh List built from them
#                                    in that same order
#   ["CallHost", name, argc]     -- pops argc values (written order),
#                                    dispatches to the REAL host function
#                                    `name` via self_hosting/lib/interp
#                                    .patlang's own interp_call_host
#                                    (reused as-is, not reimplemented --
#                                    see interp.patlang's own include
#                                    comment), pushes its result. This is
#                                    the generic fallback for any Call
#                                    whose callee isn't one of this
#                                    engine's own individually-recognized
#                                    builtins (print/box_*/etc.) and isn't
#                                    a declared block-model function
#                                    either -- see lower.patlang's own
#                                    Phase 18 note for why a call to a
#                                    DECLARED function still can't use
#                                    this (no call-with-return exists for
#                                    ordinary functions, only for Emit's
#                                    own narrow handler-dispatch case).
#                                    `["Index"]` (`lst[i]`) lowers to an
#                                    ordinary `["CallHost", "list_get", 2]`
#                                    through this SAME mechanism, not a
#                                    separate instruction.

# Optimization (post-Phase-8, measured in docs/reports/block-ownership-
# model-speed-comparison.md): a small, fast string->int hash, used to
# replace bi_run_block's own long opcode string-comparison chain with
# integer comparisons. NOT self_hosting/lib/x64_runtime.patlang's own
# hash_string -- that one runs full 64-bit FNV and returns a HEX STRING
# (meant for content-identity hashing), which would mean comparing
# 16-character strings instead of 3-15 character opcode names -- slower,
# not faster, checked before assuming otherwise. This one uses small
# multiplier/modulus values deliberately (33 and 1000003, not FNV's
# 64-bit prime) specifically to stay within ordinary tagged-int range for
# short strings and avoid tripping PatLang's own BigInt-promotion path,
# which would make the hash itself slower than the string comparison it
# replaces. Collision-checked directly against every opcode/operator
# string this engine actually uses (self_hosting/block_model/bench/
# hash_check.patlang) before trusting it for dispatch -- 32 names, 0
# collisions. Computed ONCE per instruction at LOWERING time
# (lower.patlang's own bm_hash_rewrite_instrs), never at dispatch time --
# hashing fresh on every dispatch would cost as much as the string
# comparison it's meant to avoid.
make a function called bm_op_hash takes s returns h
  let hash = 5381
  let n = s.length
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let hash = (hash * 33 + char_code(s, i)) % 1000003
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return hash
end

make a function called bi_block_name takes blk returns name
  return blk[1]
end

make a function called bi_block_params takes blk returns params
  return blk[2]
end

make a function called bi_block_instrs takes blk returns instrs
  return blk[3]
end

make a function called bi_program_entry takes prog returns name
  return prog[1]
end

make a function called bi_program_blocks takes prog returns blocks
  return prog[2]
end

# Phase 10: the compile-time event table -- `[]` for any program built
# before this phase (a plain length check, not an assumption every caller
# was updated) since bm_lower_program is the only real constructor and it
# always includes this element now, but a hand-built BlockProgramIR from a
# fixture/tool predating Phase 10 should degrade gracefully, not crash.
make a function called bi_program_events takes prog returns events
  if list_len(prog) > 3 then
    return list_get(prog, 3)
  end
  return []
end

make a function called bi_find_block takes prog, name returns blk
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let n = list_len(blocks)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let blk = list_get(blocks, i)
    if bi_block_name(blk) == name then
      return blk
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end
